    <peripheral>
      <name>I2C_ANA_MST</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C master
        Base 0x6000e000. The HAL defines only 0x6000e040 (`I2C_MST_ANA_CONF0_REG`), bits
        17 and 18 of 0x6000e044 (`ANA_CONFIG_REG`) and bit 16 of 0x6000e048
        (`ANA_CONFIG2_REG`)
        (`modules/hal/espressif/components/soc/esp32s3/include/soc/regi2c_defs.h:12-26`).
        The names I2C0_CTRL and I2C1_CTRL are the ESP32-C6 HAL's for its command
        registers.

        **Frequency hop engine** (*speculative* field meanings, *decompile* flow). The S3
        PHY can tune Wi-Fi channels with a hardware I2C sequencer in this block instead of
        a software calibration. `set_channel_rfpll_freq()` takes this path when
        `phy_param + 0x120` bit 5 is set, which `get_rf_freq_init()` does at PHY init
        after filling an 85-entry table (2400 to 2484 MHz, capacitor codes interpolated
        between calibrations at 2400 and 2464 MHz); otherwise it calls
        `set_rf_freq_offset()`. So `set_chanfreq()` on a channel frequency probably loads a
        table entry without a fresh VCO calibration (S3 `phy_rfpll.o`, `phy_hw_freq.o`).
        Its registers are I2C_ANA_MST_HOP_CTRL_REG, I2C_ANA_MST_R130_REG,
        I2C_ANA_MST_HOP_WDATA_REG, I2C_ANA_MST_R150_REG and I2C_ANA_MST_R170_REG.
      </description>
      <baseAddress>0x6000e000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>0x174</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>0,1</dimIndex>
          <name>I2C%s_CTRL</name>
          <description>
            Command word, master 0 (I2C0) and master 1 (I2C1)
            One command word per access, then poll I2C_ANA_MST_BUSY: block, register and
            bit 26 for a read (0x04000000 base), plus data and I2C_ANA_MST_WRITE for a
            write (0x05000000 base). The result is in bits 23:16.
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 ROM `rom_chip_i2c_readReg_org` (0x400354fc),
            `rom_chip_i2c_writeReg`; S3 `phy_i2c.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B26</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; always set.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>
                Busy, polled after each command.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[25:25]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>WRITE</name>
              <description>
                Set for a write.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DATA</name>
              <description>
                Data to write, or the read result.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>REG_ADDR</name>
              <description>
                Register.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BLOCK</name>
              <description>
                Block.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>ANA_CONFIG</name>
          <description>
            Readback enable, one bit per block
            The HAL's `ANA_CONFIG_REG`. Before a read the ROM writes the inverse of the
            block's bit (clear = readback enabled).
            Evidence: *decompile* ROM table at 0x3ff193dc (`rom_get_i2c_read_mask`),
            `rom_chip_i2c_readReg_org`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0x44</addressOffset>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ULP</name>
              <description>
                Block 0x61 (`i2c_ulp`); clear = readback enabled.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[22:22]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR</name>
              <description>
                Block 0x69 (`i2c_sar`); clear = readback enabled.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BBPLL</name>
              <description>
                Block 0x66 (`i2c_bbpll`); clear = readback enabled.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CKGEN</name>
              <description>
                Block 0x65 (`i2c_ckgen`); clear = readback enabled.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BLK68</name>
              <description>
                Block 0x68; clear = readback enabled.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DIG</name>
              <description>
                Block 0x6d (`i2c_dig_reg`); clear = readback enabled.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BIAS</name>
              <description>
                Block 0x6a (`i2c_bias`); clear = readback enabled.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
