    <peripheral>
      <name>ULP</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x61 (ULP)
        One bit of this block takes part in the RC calibration of block 0x6a.
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6100</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>9</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>R8</name>
          <description>
            Bit 2 set during the RC time-constant measurement
          </description>
          <addressOffset>8</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R8_B2</name>
              <description>
                Set for the RC time-constant measurement in `get_rc_dout()`, cleared after
                (see block 0x6a).
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_analog_cal.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>RFPLL</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x62 (RF PLL)
        The RF PLL's VCO and its calibration. The library reads RFPLL_VCO_WINDOW after
        every tune. The capacitor code is `0x62:5 | 0x62:7[2] &lt;&lt; 8` (9 bits; the vendor
        clamps to 0x1ff); a higher code means a lower VCO frequency.

        - After `set_rf_freq_offset()` the code is a manual one: `rfpll_cap_init_cal()`
          tries about 10 codes on either side of the calibrated one and keeps the average
          of those that read in-window (*decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`).
        - The Wi-Fi PHY's temperature tracking (`rfpll_cap_track()`, after a change of 10
          temperature units by default) reruns `rfpll_cap_correct()`, so the code can move
          after a tune (*decompile* S3 `phy_track.o`).
        - Upstream esp-sdr does a manual 512-code search on the ESP32-S2 through the same
          fields (`0x62:1`, `0x62:2[4]`, `0x62:11[6]`, `0x62:0[7]`, and `0x62:12[3:2]` as
          the window indication); it does not apply it to the S3 or the C6 (*upstream*
          `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32s2/pll.h:8-36`).
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6200</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>13</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>R0</name>
          <description>
            VCO calibration start
            Init: 0xa8, written by the vendor's PHY init

            `restart_cal()` starts the VCO calibration with a rising edge on bit 5 while
            bit 6 is toggled: it sets bit 6, pulses bit 5, clears bit 6. Bit 6 and the
            other bits: unknown.
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`, S3 `phy_i2c.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R0_B6</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; set around the pulse on RFPLL_CAL_START.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CAL_START</name>
              <description>
                Calibration start: a rising edge starts the VCO calibration.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R1</name>
          <description>
            Manual capacitor code, bits 7:0
          </description>
          <addressOffset>1</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MANUAL_CODE</name>
              <description>
                `write_pll_cap()` writes the code here (`rf_test` prints it as `ir_cap_ext`).
                After a tune it holds the low byte of the code in use: 127 with code 383,
                208 at 2412 MHz.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`, `rf_test.o`; *measured* bench
                2026-10-09
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R2</name>
          <description>
            4-bit code override and manual capacitor code bit 8
            Init: 0x88, written by the vendor's PHY init

            `get_rf_freq_init()` clears RFPLL_CODE4_OVR, calibrates at 2437 MHz, copies
            RFPLL_CODE4_CAL into RFPLL_CODE4_VAL and sets RFPLL_CODE4_OVR. What the 4-bit
            code controls is unknown.
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_hw_freq.o`, `phy_i2c.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>2</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CODE4_OVR</name>
              <description>
                4-bit code override: cleared before and set after the copy into
                RFPLL_CODE4_VAL.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MANUAL_CODE_B8</name>
              <description>
                Manual capacitor code, bit 8. Written with RFPLL_MANUAL_CODE.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CODE4_VAL</name>
              <description>
                The 4-bit code, copied from RFPLL_CODE4_CAL.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R5</name>
          <description>
            Capacitor code readback, bits 7:0
          </description>
          <addressOffset>5</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CAP_CODE</name>
              <description>
                Capacitor code readback, bits 7:0. Values: see "Measured PLL behaviour".
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`; *measured*
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R6</name>
          <description>
            Calibrated 4-bit code
          </description>
          <addressOffset>6</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CODE4_CAL</name>
              <description>
                Copied into RFPLL_CODE4_VAL by the vendor.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_hw_freq.o`, `phy_i2c.o` (as RFPLL_R2)
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R7</name>
          <description>
            Capacitor code bit 8 and calibration done
          </description>
          <addressOffset>7</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CAP_CODE_B8</name>
              <description>
                Capacitor code readback, bit 8.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`; *measured*
              </description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CAL_DONE</name>
              <description>
                Calibration done; 1 after every tune. `wait_rfpll_cal_end()` polls it every
                20 us, 100 times, then prints `error: pll_cal exceeds 2ms!!!`.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`; *measured*
              </description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>R7_B0</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; 1 in every read (RFPLL_R7 = 0x03 or 0x07).
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09
              </description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R11</name>
          <description>
            Capacitor code source
            Init: 0x48, written by the vendor's PHY init
          </description>
          <addressOffset>11</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CODE_SRC</name>
              <description>
                Cleared before each software calibration (`set_rfpll_freq()`), set by
                `rfpll_cap_init_cal()` before it tries manual codes. Inferred: 0 = code from
                the calibration engine, 1 = code from RFPLL_MANUAL_CODE and
                RFPLL_MANUAL_CODE_B8.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R12</name>
          <description>
            VCO window comparator and two unknown status bits
            Init: 0x00, read at 2412 MHz (see "Measured PLL behaviour")
          </description>
          <addressOffset>12</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>VCO_WINDOW</name>
              <description>
                After calibration: 0 = the VCO inside its tuning window; 1 = target below
                the window (code pinned at its maximum); 2 = target above it (code 0). Live
                for the current code: the vendor's `rfpll_cap_correct()` moves the code in
                steps of 2, up while it reads 1 and down while it reads 2.
                `esp_sdr_set_freq()` and `esp_sdr_set_freq_offset()` return -ERANGE when it
                is not 0. Window (PLL): 2193 to 2781 MHz.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09; *working*
                `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr.c:78-97, 194-196, 213-215`; *decompile* S3
                `phy_rfpll.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>R12_B1</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; set from about 2700 MHz (PLL) up.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09
              </description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>R12_B0</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; always 0.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09
              </description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>RFPLL_SDM</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x63 (RF PLL fractional-N divider)
        The S3 sets the PLL frequency through registers 0 and 3 to 5:

        `f_PLL = 0.75 * f_xtal * (32 + r3 + r4 / 2^8 + r5 / 2^16)`

        With the 40 MHz crystal that is 30 MHz per integer step and about 458 Hz per
        LSB. `write_rfpll_sdm()` writes reg 0 = 0x07, regs 3 to 5, then reg 0 = 0x17
        (bit 4 presumably loads the word).

        | PLL MHz | reg 3 | reg 4 | reg 5 | Decoded |
        |---|---|---|---|---|
        | 2412 | 0x30 | 0x66 | 0x66 | 30 MHz x 80.4 |
        | 2500 | 0x33 | 0x55 | 0x55 | 30 MHz x 83.33 |
        | 2700 | 0x3a | 0x00 | 0x00 | 30 MHz x 90 |

        Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o` (`rfpll_set_freq()`), S3 `phy_rfpll.o`
        (`write_rfpll_sdm()`); *measured* bench 2026-10-09: the raw values match the
        formula exactly.

        The crystal selector of the vendor calls picks the divisor, as on the C6: 1 =
        26 MHz, 2 = 32 MHz, anything else = 40 MHz (*decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o`).
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6300</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>6</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>R0</name>
          <description>
            Divider word load (presumed)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD</name>
              <description>
                Presumably loads the word: `write_rfpll_sdm()` writes the register as 0x07
                before registers 3 to 5 and as 0x17 after them.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_rfpll.o` (`write_rfpll_sdm()`)
              </description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R3</name>
          <description>
            Integer part above 32
          </description>
          <addressOffset>3</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>INTEGER</name>
              <description>
                `r3` in the formula above: 30 MHz per step with the 40 MHz crystal.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R4</name>
          <description>
            Fraction, high byte
          </description>
          <addressOffset>4</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FRAC_H</name>
              <description>
                `r4` in the formula above, weight 1 / 2^8.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R5</name>
          <description>
            Fraction, low byte
          </description>
          <addressOffset>5</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FRAC_L</name>
              <description>
                `r5` in the formula above, weight 1 / 2^16: about 458 Hz per LSB with the
                40 MHz crystal.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>CKGEN</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x65 (clock generator, LO 5/6 divider)
        Register 0 bit 4 (mask 0x10) selects the LO 5/6 divider. The ESP32-C6 has no
        block 0x65; its selector is in block 0x62.

        - The S3 libphy and librftest objects only read block 0x65 in their debug dump
          (*decompile* S3 `phy_debug.o`).
        - Upstream qualifies the selector on the ESP32, S2, S3, C2, C3 and C6 and warns
          that the C5, C61, H2 and S31 lay out their analog blocks differently
          (`modules/lib/esp-sdr/docs/rx-controls.md:102-107`).
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6500</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>9</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>R0</name>
          <description>
            LO 5/6 divider select

            - Change only the bit (read-modify-write). Clear it before the vendor tuning
              call, which calibrates in normal mode; set it after the receive or transmit
              front-end setup.
            - Without the select on the transmit side, transmissions in the divider band
              went out at 6/5 of the target.
            - The library uses the divider for every frequency below 2210 MHz (PLL at 1.2
              times the LO), upstream only from 1842 MHz.

            Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr.c:106-108`,
            `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:92-95`, `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_tx.c:25-26, 77-83`
            (read-modify-write and order)
            Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-09 (6/5 of the target on transmit)
            Evidence: `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr.c:65-76`,
            `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/rx_lo.h:15-19` (divider band)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>0</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LO_DIV</name>
              <description>
                0: the LO is the PLL. 1: LO = PLL / 1.2 (5/6 conversion). One selector feeds
                both the receive and the transmit mixer.
                Evidence: *upstream* `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/rx_lo.h:47-62`,
                external-tone tests `modules/lib/esp-sdr/docs/rx-controls.md:76-100`;
                *measured* on air for receive and transmit (bench 2026-10-09)
              </description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>BBPLL</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x66 (BBPLL)
        Documented by the HAL. Upstream's never-released `SAMPLE_RATE_PROBE` builds write
        `0x66:4[3:2]` (`I2C_BBPLL_DIV_ADC`) around a capture to look for other ADC rates
        and record no result (*upstream*
        `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/targets/esp32s3/receiver.c:140-155`). esp_sdr does not
        touch the block.
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6600</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>11</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>BBTOP</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x67 (BBTOP, analog baseband)
        esp_sdr writes the receive and transmit filter codes here.

        - BBTOP_R4 and BBTOP_R6 both reading 0x10 is the vendor's sign that BBTOP lost
          its state; it then reruns its init (*decompile* S3 `phy_i2c.o`).
        - Writing the other pairs (0x06/0x07 up to 0x1e/0x1f) disturbed reception until a
          reboot (*measured* bench 2026-10-06). `esp_sdr_bbtop_read()` and
          `esp_sdr_bbtop_write()` give unchecked access
          (`lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:648-667`).
        - The PHY's channel bandwidth mode changes these filters too (hardware-quirks.md,
          "40 MHz mode widens the analog filters").

        Registers 0x24 to 0x38 (36 to 56) take init constants, bits 7:0 (*decompile* S3
        `phy_i2c.o`):

        | Registers | Value |
        |---|---|
        | 0x24, 0x28 | 0x48 |
        | 0x25, 0x29 | 0x08 |
        | 0x2c, 0x2d, 0x30, 0x31 | 0x88 |
        | 0x34, 0x35 | 0x11 |
        | 0x38 | 0xff |
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6700</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>57</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>R2</name>
          <description>
            Unknown 2-bit field, 1 at init
            `phy_close_rf()` writes the register as 6.
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_i2c.o`, `phy_init.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>2</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R2_B3_2</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; 1 at init.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R3</name>
          <description>
            Unknown bit, changed by the receive calibration
            The S3 frequency hop engine (see "Analog I2C master") writes the register as
            0xf0 and 0xf4.
            Evidence: *speculative* S3 `phy_rx_cal.o`, `phy_hw_freq.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>3</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R3_B2</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; saved, changed and restored by the receive calibration.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>1</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>4,5</dimIndex>
          <name>R%s</name>
          <description>
            Receive low-pass capacitor code, I (BBTOP_R4) and Q (BBTOP_R5)
            Vendor scaling: the corner goes as 1 / (code + 8). `phy_rx_band_set(1, bw)`
            writes `clamp((base + 8) * 19 / min(bw, 19) - 8, 2, 60)` to both; `base` comes
            from the RC calibration (block 0x6a).
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_api.o` (vendor scaling)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>4</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RX_LPF</name>
              <description>
                Receive low-pass capacitor code, 0 to 63. Larger is narrower; 0 is the widest
                setting, not a bypass: about 69 MHz (0) to 13 MHz (60) two-sided, PHY default
                about 22 MHz. Bits 7:6 are kept.
                Evidence: *working* `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:126-148`; *upstream* curves
                `modules/lib/esp-sdr/main/common/rx_bandwidth.h:79-84`; *measured* (S3
                default) bench 2026-10-05
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>1</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>6,7</dimIndex>
          <name>R%s</name>
          <description>
            Second capacitor pair, unknown
            Probably a second receive stage. Written at init and, with BBTOP_R14 and
            BBTOP_R15 (0x0e, 0x0f), for 10 and 5 MHz channels. The library leaves it alone.
            Evidence: *speculative*; *decompile* S3 `phy_i2c.o`, `phy_feature.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>6</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CAP2</name>
              <description>
                Capacitor code, unknown.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>1</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>12,13</dimIndex>
          <name>R%s</name>
          <description>
            Transmit low-pass pair A (0x0c, 0x0d)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>12</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TX_LPF_A</name>
              <description>
                Transmit low-pass code, pair A. 63 narrows TX to about +-6 MHz; codes below
                the calibrated one (about 35) do not widen it.
                Evidence: *measured* bench 2026-10-06; *decompile* S3 `rf_test.o`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>2</dim>
          <dimIncrement>1</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>14,15</dimIndex>
          <name>R%s</name>
          <description>
            Transmit low-pass pair B (0x0e, 0x0f)
          </description>
          <addressOffset>14</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TX_LPF_B</name>
              <description>
                Transmit low-pass code, pair B.
                Evidence: *decompile* S3 `rf_test.o`; *working*
                `lib/esp_sdr/src/esp_sdr_rx.c:578-637`
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>4</dim>
          <dimIncrement>1</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>20,21,22,23</dimIndex>
          <name>R%s</name>
          <description>
            Unknown pairs (0x14 to 0x17), written from PHY parameters at init
            Evidence: *speculative* S3 `phy_i2c.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>20</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B5_0</name>
              <description>
                Unknown.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>4</dim>
          <dimIncrement>1</dimIncrement>
          <dimIndex>28,29,30,31</dimIndex>
          <name>R%s</name>
          <description>
            Unknown pairs (0x1c to 0x1f), written from PHY parameters at init
            Stepped with BBTOP_R12 and BBTOP_R13 (0x0c, 0x0d) in a TX DC calibration test.
            Evidence: *speculative* S3 `phy_i2c.o`, `phy_test.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>28</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>B5_0</name>
              <description>
                Unknown.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>BIAS</name>
      <description>
        Analog I2C block 0x6a (bias): RC calibration
        Not used by esp_sdr. `get_rc_dout(mode)` measures an on-chip RC time constant: it
        writes `0x6a:2[6:5]` = 2, `0x6a:6[4:0]` = 2, `0x6a:4[7:4]` = 7, 11 or 6 by mode,
        `0x61:8[2]` = 1 and `0x6a:4[0]` = 1, pulses `0x6a:4[3]`, waits 100 us, reads
        `0x6a:5[5:0]` and clears `0x61:8[2]` and `0x6a:4[0]`. `rc_cal()` turns the result
        into the receive filter base code, `clamp((dout + 56) * 82 / 190 - 8, 2, 63)`, so
        the filter codes follow process spread. Evidence: *decompile* S3
        `phy_analog_cal.o`.
      </description>
      <groupName>ANALOG_I2C</groupName>
      <baseAddress>0x6a00</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>8</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>R2</name>
          <description>
            RC measurement setting in bits 6:5
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_analog_cal.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>2</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R2_B6_5</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; `get_rc_dout()` writes 2.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[6:5]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R4</name>
          <description>
            RC measurement mode and control bits
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_analog_cal.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>4</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R4_B7_4</name>
              <description>
                `get_rc_dout(mode)` writes 7, 11 or 6 by mode.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>R4_B3</name>
              <description>
                Pulsed by `get_rc_dout()`, which then waits 100 us and reads BIAS_RC_DOUT.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>R4_B0</name>
              <description>
                Set by `get_rc_dout()` before the pulse on BIAS_R4_B3, cleared after the
                read.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R5</name>
          <description>
            RC measurement result
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_analog_cal.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>5</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RC_DOUT</name>
              <description>
                The RC time-constant measurement, read by `get_rc_dout()` 100 us after the
                pulse on BIAS_R4_B3; `rc_cal()` turns it into the receive filter base code.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>R6</name>
          <description>
            RC measurement setting in bits 4:0
            Evidence: *decompile* S3 `phy_analog_cal.o`
          </description>
          <addressOffset>6</addressOffset>
          <size>8</size>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R6_B4_0</name>
              <description>
                Unknown; `get_rc_dout()` writes 2.
              </description>
              <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
  </peripherals>
</device>
